dialect protocol
protocol stdio.csv.mutations
version 1
schema stdio.csv.op
start op

# Real binary op-frame `protocol::OpBinary::encode_op`/`decode_op` NOW emit for CsvMutation —
# upgraded, this program, from the F6-era `print_op().into_bytes()` text-as-binary shortcut
# (see this artifact's report). `tag u8` is the mutation's own ordinal (this enum cannot use
# `#[derive(dsl::DslOps)]`, see 🧬️mutations/🦀️.rs's own doc comment, so the ordinal is
# hand-assigned, declaration order: SetSnapshot=1, SetHasHeader=2,
# InsertRecord=3, RemoveRecord=4, SetField=5 — `NoMutation` was dropped, ordinal 0 retired, not
# reassigned). Every scalar field below (the ordinal, `index`,
# `record_index`/`field_index`, `has_header`, `quoted`) is genuinely, individually byte-walked
# and validated. Each arm's own trailing `bytes` field is this artifact's hand-rolled nested
# `CsvSnapshot`/`CsvRecord`/string binary encoding — deliberately placed LAST in every arm so it
# can honestly consume "rest of buffer" with no length prefix needed. The deeply nested,
# variable-shaped collection INSIDE that tail (a record's own field list, or a whole snapshot's
# record list) is genuinely NOT expressible by this dialect's `Array` (homogeneous
# single-`Prim`-element repeat only) or `Ref` (struct/enum references still unconditionally
# error at walk time, confirmed unchanged since P2-M2) — see this artifact's report,
# mechanism_gaps "csv-nested-record-array-unwalkable". Same honest-opaque-tail treatment the
# user's own carve-out already gives a compressed payload.
framing record
repeat op {
tag u8
arm 1 { snapshot bytes }
arm 2 { has_header u8 }
arm 3 { index varint record bytes }
arm 4 { index varint }
arm 5 { record_index varint field_index varint quoted u8 value bytes }
}
